# Target C: close the eight-no-root `k=5` low-`p` branches by lifting five-row slack-two containment

Date: 2026-09-23

Status: rigorous proof-level closure of two of the five arithmetic branches in the eight-no-root `k=5` layer. This uses the certified five-no-root rank-three slack-two theorem. No new exhaustive computation is used. It does not close the other three `k=5` branches, Target C-II, or Erdos Problem #99, and it does not change canonical theorem-state authority.

Inputs:

- `research/target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_eight_noroot_k6_layer_closure_k5_two_clean_reduction_2026-09-23.md`, which reduces the `k=5` layer to five arithmetic branches and gives `rank(P)=3`;
- `research/verify_target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_five_noroot_k2_slack_2026_09_20.py`, whose certified five-row theorem says that for a rank-three five-row coefficient space obtainable from empty-block constraints plus at most two singleton-block constraints, the virtual complete-block deficiency is at least one and every external nullspace relation lies in the virtual complete-block relation span.

The preceding branch-specific note

`research/target_c_support7_global_segment_relative_eight_noroot_k5_dminus2_d1p2_sharp_singletons_2026-09-23.md`

remains valid, but the new lift below makes its detailed touch/coset endpoint unnecessary for closing the `p=2` branch.

## 1. Rank-three eight-row setup

Let

`B=R union E`, `|E|=8`,

be an eight-no-root state with

```text
k(B)=5
```

and let

`P <= Q^8`

be its external coefficient-constraint space. Then

```text
rank(P)=3.
```

Put

`N = null(P) <= Q^8`.

Hence

```text
dim N = 5.
```

Assume throughout this note that the total selected incomplete-root cost satisfies

```text
p<=2.
```

An incomplete singleton block contributes at most one local coefficient-constraint line; a pair or complete state contributes none; empty blocks contribute their empty-state constraint spaces. Therefore the actual eight-row realization obtains `P` from empty-block constraints plus at most two singleton-block constraints.

For a three-set `J subset E`, call `J` **good** when deleting the coordinates in `J` preserves `rank(P)=3`. Let

```text
P_J = pi_J(P),
N_J = {alpha in N : alpha_j=0 for all j in J}.
```

For good `J`, extension by zero on `J` identifies `N_J` with `null(P_J)`, so

```text
dim N_J = 2.
```

The five-row punctured actual state still uses at most the same two singleton incomplete blocks. Consequently `P_J` is obtainable from projected empty-block constraints plus at most two projected singleton constraints, and the certified five-row slack-two theorem applies to every good `J`.

## 2. Every good triple supplies deficiency and external containment

Fix a good triple `J`.

Let `C_J` be the virtual-canonical complete-block set of the five-row puncture. The certified five-row theorem gives

```text
d_J = |C_J| - rank H(C_J) >= 1,               (2.1)
```

and

```text
L_J(N_J) subset H(C_J),                        (2.2)
```

where `L_J` denotes the canonical external-relation map in the punctured state.

Let `C` be the eight-row virtual-canonical complete-block set relative to `P`. If a block is globally virtual-empty, its empty constraint is contained in `P`, so after puncturing its projected empty constraint is contained in `P_J`. Therefore

```text
C_J subset C.                                  (2.3)
```

The containment (2.2) lifts to the eight-row relation space for every `alpha in N_J`.

Indeed, take a block `b in C\C_J`. If `b` becomes untouched after deleting `J`, every deleted contribution is multiplied by an `alpha`-coordinate in `J`, hence vanishes because `alpha_J=0`. If `b` remains touched but becomes virtual-empty, its projected empty constraint is contained in `P_J`, so the punctured null vector `alpha` annihilates its local quotient demand. Thus restoring the deleted coordinates adds no demand at any block omitted from `C_J`.

Hence

```text
L(alpha) in H(C)                                (2.4)
```

for every `alpha in N_J` and every good triple `J`.

## 3. Coordinate-basis triples span the full nullspace

Let

```text
Q = Q^8 / P.
```

Since `dim Q=5` and the eight coordinate classes span `Q`, choose five coordinates

```text
B_0={i_1,...,i_5}
```

whose classes

```text
e_{i_r}+P
```

form a basis of `Q`.

Every three-subset `J subset B_0` is good. A rank loss on such a deletion would mean that `P` contains a nonzero vector supported on `J`, equivalently that the three corresponding coordinate classes in `Q` are linearly dependent. They are members of a basis, so this cannot happen.

Use the perfect pairing

```text
Q x N -> Q,
(x+P, alpha) -> alpha dot x.
```

The five coordinate classes from `B_0` give a basis of `N^*`. In the resulting dual coordinate system on `N`, for a three-subset `J subset B_0`, the space `N_J` is exactly the two-dimensional coordinate plane obtained by setting the three coordinates indexed by `J` to zero.

As `J` ranges over the ten three-subsets of `B_0`, these two-dimensional planes span all of `N`: every coordinate axis lies in one of them. Therefore

```text
N = span( N_J : J subset B_0, |J|=3 ).         (3.1)
```

Combining (2.4) and (3.1), and using linearity of the canonical external-relation map,

```text
L(N) subset H(C).                               (3.2)
```

Thus the full eight-row external-containment property follows from the five-row slack-two theorem.

## 4. The global virtual deficiency is at least one

For any three-subset `J subset B_0`, equations (2.1) and (2.3) give a punctured complete set `C_J subset C` with deficiency at least one.

The complete-block relation rows are the same root-block `T` relations before and after puncturing. Adding complete-block rows cannot reduce row nullity. Hence

```text
d = |C| - rank H(C) >= d_J >= 1.               (4.1)
```

## 5. Low-`p` global defect bound

Let `q` be the number of globally virtual-complete blocks changed to incomplete states in the actual eight-row state.

A globally virtual-complete block cannot be actual-empty, because its empty constraint is not contained in `P`. Every changed virtual-complete block therefore uses at least one selected incomplete-root row, so

```text
q<=p.                                           (5.1)
```

The standard virtual-canonical accounting is valid globally because of (3.2). With eight no-root rows,

```text
D(B) >= 8 + p + 3d - 3q.                        (5.2)
```

Using (4.1) and (5.1),

```text
D(B) >= 8 + p + 3 - 3p
     = 11 - 2p.                                  (5.3)
```

Therefore every eight-no-root rank-three state with `p<=2` satisfies

```text
p=0 -> D>=11,
p=1 -> D>=9,
p=2 -> D>=7.
```

In particular, no such state can have negative defect.

## 6. Two `k=5` arithmetic branches close

The exact `k=5` arithmetic boundary is

```text
D=-2: (d_root,p)=(0,5),(1,2)
D=-1: (d_root,p)=(0,6),(1,3),(2,0).
```

Equation (5.3) excludes both low-`p` branches:

```text
D=-2, (d_root,p)=(1,2),
D=-1, (d_root,p)=(2,0).
```

Hence only three eight-no-root `k=5` branches remain:

```text
D=-2, (d_root,p)=(0,5),
D=-1, (d_root,p)=(0,6),
D=-1, (d_root,p)=(1,3).
```

This strictly improves the preceding five-branch frontier.

## 7. Reusable lift principle

The proof isolates a general mechanism that can be reused below `k=5`.

Suppose an `n`-coordinate coefficient space `P` has quotient dimension `m`, and a certified puncture theorem gives external containment after deleting every `r`-subset of some coordinate basis of `Q^n/P`. If those punctured nullspaces have dimension `m-r` and their coordinate subspaces span the parent nullspace, then containment lifts globally, provided blocks omitted from the punctured virtual-complete set have zero demand on the punctured null vectors.

Here

```text
m=5,
r=3,
m-r=2,
```

and all ten three-subsets of a five-coordinate quotient basis are available.

No new verifier result, finite census, or unexecuted code is used as evidence in this closure.
